  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening the resilience of the most vulnerable population in the underserved areas of Rural Damascus</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">ltpgtltbrgtThe number of people expected to need assistance in Syria has increased and the socioeconomic situation has continued to deteriorate as a result of the war, as well as the post-effects of the February 6 earthquake, which have increased the risks to the most vulnerable communities and affected their living standards and resilience.ltbrgtSince the end of the war, the Syria Trust for Development has intervened in a variety of humanitarian sectors in the Douma region, which has provided a deeper understanding of the humanitarian context and enabled it to identify priorities for intervention in the area with the participation of the local community.ltbrgtDouma Municipality, located 10 km from Damascus in Rural Damascus Governorate, is composed of 7 districts and includes 14 neighborhoods. It has a population of approximately 300,000 and about 60,000 families.ltbrgtWith stability returning, economic activities are resuming in agriculture, livestock, crafts, and retail. Efforts by residents, government agencies, and international organizations have resulted in the renovation of key institutions, service centers, facilities, and schools, including the filling of tunnels.ltbrgtRural Damascus governorate, heavily impacted by the war, requires urgent intervention in Douma according to the priorities indicated by the local community, and the Trust team assessed the Rehabilitation of 100 damaged apartments and the common area of 10 buildings in Douma City.ltbrgtThe plan aims to provide safe shelter for approximately 100 families in Douma.ltbrgtlt/pgt</narrative>
  </description>
